ns.\n\nCandidate B: very conscientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills, weak memory for numbers, makes nasty remarks about colleagues. Strengths: reliability, stress resilience, weather assessment. Weaknesses: interpersonal (nasty remarks), memory for numbers (could affect calculations). Communication? Not mentioned but nasty remarks suggests poor crew cooperation.\n\nCandidate C: anticipates dangerous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership, regarded as show-off, not open to new ideas. Strengths: situational awareness, technical cognition, leadership. Weaknesses: attitude (show-off, not open), could affect crew cooperation.\n\nCandidate D: responds adequately to unexpected events, concentrates well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, regarded as know-it-all, hot temper. Strengths: problem solving, responsibility, concentration. Weaknesses: hot temper, know-it-all attitude (cooperation issues).\n\nWe need to pick best overall fit. Which candidate balances strengths and minimal concerns? Let's weigh.\n\nA: strong decision, calm, technical, but communication and egocentric are serious for crew. Likely disqualify.\n\nB: strong reliability, stress, weather, but nasty remarks and weak memory for numbers are concerning for safety. Memory for numbers important for flight plans. Might be a red flag.\n\nC: strong situational awareness, leadership, technical, but not open to new ideas and show-off attitude could affect crew cooperation. Still maybe okay? Leadership is good, but attitude may be problematic.\n\nD: strong problem solving, responsibility, concentration, but hot temper and know-it-all attitude could affect crew under stress. Hot temper is serious.\n\nWhich is best? Possibly Candidate C: high technical competence, leadership, anticipates danger, but attitude concerns. Candidate B has interpersonal issues too. C's not open to new ideas may be less severe than B's nasty remarks. D's hot temper is severe. A's communication issues severe.\n\nThus I might recommend Candidate C, but note concerns. Provide evidence: anticipates dangerous situations, leadership, but show-off and not open to new ideas.\n\nLet's produce message: state recommendation for Candidate C, explain why based on criteria, mention strengths and concerns, and note that compared to others, C's technical and leadership outweigh attitude concerns.\n\nProceed."}
